data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing is a genetic engineering technique that modifies living organisms' genomes, based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- Delivering Cas9 nuclease with a synthetic guide RNA (gRNA) into a cell lets the genome be cut at a chosen location, allowing genes to be removed or new ones added [1].\n- Cas9 is a DNA-cutting endonuclease that makes site-specific double-stranded breaks, enabling gene inactivation, removal, or insertion via cellular repair mechanisms such as non-homologous end joining [3].\n- CRISPR sequences in prokaryotes are derived from bacteriophage DNA and are used to detect and destroy DNA from similar phages during later infections [2].\n- The CRISPR-Cas9 system is widely used in genetic engineering, medicine, agriculture, and biotechnology [3]."}

